WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:08.141
[Michael]<i> Stanford University,
 1973.</i>

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.642
<i> Professor Philip Zimbardo
 conducts</i>

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:11.411
<i> one of the most infamous
 experiments</i>

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:13.646
<i> in the history
 of psychology.</i>

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:16.249
<i> Known as the Stanford
 Prison Experiment,</i>

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:19.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> this dark study
 of human behavior
 had student volunteers</i>

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:22.688
<i> acting out the roles of
 prison guards and inmates.</i>

00:00:22.721 --> 00:00:25.358
<i> But the role-playing soon
 became all too real.</i>

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:28.327 line:0%
[man shouting]

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.096
<i> Subjects in the role
 of prison guards</i>

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:32.065
<i> became domineering
 and hostile.</i>

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.800 line:0%
[participants shouting]

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.740
<i> Those in the role of inmates
 felt humiliation and fear.</i>

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.414
<i> The experiment was scheduled
 to last two weeks,</i>

00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:50.650
<i> but after spiraling
 out of control,</i>

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.218 line:92%
<i> this controversial study</i>

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:55.822
<i> was abruptly stopped
 after just six days.</i>

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.624
Why? Because someone
was heroic enough

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:01.794
to stand up against
what they thought was wrong.

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:05.364
That person convinced
Zimbardo to end the experiment.

00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:08.801
The experience affected
Dr. Zimbardo so powerfully

00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:10.703
that he has since
turned his energy

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:12.405
away from the study of evil

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.774
and onto the study
of heroism.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:17.310
According to Zimbardo,
being a hero doesn't mean

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.512
you have to rescue someone
from a burning building.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:23.216
A hero is just someone
who acts selflessly,

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.817
out of concern for others,

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.586
at personal risk

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.789
and without the expectation
of reward.

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.158
It took a hero to empathize
with the subjects

00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:34.160
in the Stanford
Prison Experiment

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.529 line:0%
<i> and stand up to Dr. Zimbardo.</i>

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:38.698 line:0%
<i> But where do heroes
 like that come from?</i>

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.467
Is heroism a personality type
you are born with,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.504
or can you be taught
to be a hero?

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.573
And do you really need a cape?

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:51.144
[theme music playing]

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.694
[Michael]<i> I sat down
 with Philip Zimbardo</i>

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.930
<i> and his wife,
 Christine Maslach,</i>

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:12.765
<i> to hear about
the Stanford Prison Experiment</i>

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.667
<i> and how it brought them
 together as a couple.</i>

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.335 line:0%
The Stanford prison study

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:17.770 line:0%
was supposed to go
for two weeks,

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:20.373 line:0%
and we had to
terminate it early

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:24.844 line:0%
because of some unexpected
turbulence in the study.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:28.181 line:0%
I was just really upset
at what I was seeing,

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.782 line:0%
and, um,

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:31.517
just the sort of dehumanization

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:32.852
and-and people are watching,

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.887
and nobody's bothered by it.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.256
And it wasn't just Phil,
it was everybody else.

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.192
People were sort of
teasing me,

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.860
some of the other grad students
were just saying,

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.862
"Hey, you just got your PhD
in Psychology.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.730
This is human behavior,
right?"

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:46.566 align:start position:0%
But it's upsetting
in two ways:

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:48.868 align:start position:0%
one, that this shouldn't
be human behavior.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.304 align:start position:0%
This is so mean.
But also--

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:52.805
-It's inhuman behavior.
-It's inhuman behavior.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.707 align:start position:0%
And also,
is it also inhuman

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.642 align:start position:0%
to just watch it
and not intervene?

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:58.878
Of course.
In retrospect.

00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:02.515
No, but when you're in it,
you're part of the evil.

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:03.783
I was looking at it
through a different lens.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.384
Everybody is saying,
the graduates,

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:07.286
"Wow, this is really
interesting."

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:09.555 size:81% position:59%
...playing back the videos
during the night,

00:03:09.588 --> 00:03:11.424
and she's just saying...
[sharp whistle]

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.526 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I'm blowing the whistle
on this, you know?"

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.461 align:start size:69% position:31%
I could not understand

00:03:15.494 --> 00:03:19.432 align:start size:91% position:9%
how we were not seeing things
in the same way.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:20.399
But then the key thing
she says

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:23.536
about our relationship is...

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.005 align:start size:78% position:22%
[chuckles] Yeah.
"If this is the real you,
then we're done.

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:28.241 align:start size:44% position:56%
This is over."

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.942
And at that moment,
that was the slap in the fa--

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:32.445
That was the wakeup call.
"Hello. Wake up."

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.313
She said, "I don't think
I want to continue

00:03:34.346 --> 00:03:37.583
my relationship with you,"
and that's-that's heroic.

00:03:37.616 --> 00:03:39.018
Heroes take action,

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.487
which involves a personal risk.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.856
She said,
"Come to your senses,"

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:45.925
and, parentheses,
"Make the right decision.

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:48.527
And if you don't,
I'm out of here."

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.930 align:start position:0%
Did you make the decision
to end the experiment
that night?

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:51.764 align:start size:66% position:34%
-Oh, sure.
-Yeah.

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:00.706
<i> Being a hero</i>

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:03.509
means putting yourself
on the line to help others.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:06.479
But in the real world,
ethics can get murky,

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:09.048
and those who are celebrated
as heroes by some

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:12.018
can be denounced as villains
by others.

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:16.022 line:0%
In 1971, former military
analyst Daniel Ellsberg

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.424
<i> leaked secret documents
 he had access to</i>

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:19.825
<i> regarding the Vietnam War.</i>

00:04:19.858 --> 00:04:21.927
<i> The so-called</i> Pentagon Papers

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.763
showed that
the Johnson administration

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.299
was lying to both the media
and Congress

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.734
regarding the scope of
U.S. actions in Vietnam.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:32.405
<i> Ellsberg faced 115 years
 in prison for espionage,</i>

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.573
<i> But his case was dismissed
 due to illegal handling</i>

00:04:34.606 --> 00:04:36.342
<i> of evidence on the part
 of the government.</i>

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:39.011 line:0%
<i> More recently,
 both Chelsea Manning</i>

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:42.081 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> and Edward Snowden
 released classified
 government information.</i>

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:43.849 line:92%
<i> All three whistle-blowers</i>

00:04:43.882 --> 00:04:45.885
<i> are now seen as heroes
 by some</i>

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.387
and traitors by others.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.389
What these individuals
have in common

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:53.859
is that they all risked
their reputations and safety

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:57.029
to expose what they saw
as wrongdoing.

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:59.031
Would you be willing
to risk your job,

00:04:59.064 --> 00:05:01.534
your friends,
your reputation,

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.703
to do what you
thought was right?

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.838
Well, to find out,
let's observe

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:07.740
the whistleblower
and the bystander

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.609
in their natural habitat.

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:17.049 line:0%
<i> In a 2012 survey
 conducted by Dr. Zimbardo</i>

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:18.484 line:0%
<i> and Dr. Piero Bocchiaro,</i>

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:20.653 line:0%
<i> participants were asked
 what they would do</i>

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:23.556
<i> if they were put into
an unethical situation at work.</i>

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.957
<i> Over 95% said they would
 disobey their boss</i>

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.593
<i> and blow the whistle.</i>

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:29.829
<i> But it's one thing to say
 you'd blow the whistle,</i>

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:32.365 size:84% position:42%
<i> and another thing
 to actually do it.</i>

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.367
<i> At a local community college,</i>

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.535
<i> we set up a realistic
 whistleblower test</i>

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:37.603
<i> with the help
 of Dr. Zimbardo</i>

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:39.772
<i> and Dr. Bocchiaro.</i>

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.041
<i> Our subjects
 were temporary employees</i>

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:44.043
<i> who believed they were hired
 to help set up</i>

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:48.080
<i> a simple psychological
 experiment.</i>

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:49.448
<i> For authenticity,</i>

00:05:49.481 --> 00:05:51.851 align:start size:88% position:13%
we've gained access
to a local college's
Psychology department,

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.820
so, from the very moment
our participants arrive,

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:58.924
they will believe that our
scientific experiment is real.

00:05:58.957 --> 00:06:01.560
<i> But the experiment is fake,</i>

00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:03.929
<i> and everyone they're
 about to meet is an actor.</i>

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:05.030 align:start size:47% position:53%
You guys ready?

00:06:05.063 --> 00:06:06.665 size:88% position:44%
-Absolutely.
-So ready.

00:06:06.698 --> 00:06:08.067 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Psychology professor
 Ron Mossler</i>

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.602 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> will be observing
 the subjects</i>

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.537
<i> from our hidden control room,</i>

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.739
<i> while I play the role
 of a researcher,</i>

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:15.975
<i> a very unethical researcher.</i>

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.009
<i> First up is Sherry...</i>

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:18.911
-Hello.
-Hello.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.679
<i> ...who's accompanied
 by an actor</i>

00:06:20.712 --> 00:06:22.581
<i> posing as
 another temp worker.</i>

00:06:22.614 --> 00:06:25.151
Let me explain
what we're recruiting for.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:28.087 line:0%
I call it groundbreaking
because it's...

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.756 align:end position:100% line:16%
it's a study that
hasn't been done,
at least at this scale.

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:32.925
The study is a study
on isolation

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:34.460
and sensory deprivation.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.195 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Sensory deprivation?
-Correct.

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:38.164
We are going to be putting
people in isolation

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:40.633
for ten days.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:42.768
There won't be any windows,
no clocks,

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.870
nothing to read,
nothing to write on.

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:47.473
The lights are going to be on
the entire time,

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:49.041
and there won't be anything
in there

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:51.977
but a camping toilet.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:52.945
In three-day studies,

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:55.514
we know that anxiety

00:06:55.547 --> 00:06:57.950
and delusions
are quite common,

00:06:57.983 --> 00:07:00.085
but no one's
ever tried ten days.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:02.988
There's the potential
for nervous breakdowns.

00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:05.057
There's a lot of stress
on the individual,

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.561
and, uh, there can be
some kind of cognitive
regression, as well.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:12.097 align:start position:0%
When will you tell them
about the risks?

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.799
They'll probably be told
about the risks

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:15.167
in a debrief
after the experiment.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> -After</i> the experiment?
-Yes.

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:20.639
Don't you think that's
a little not so cool.

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:21.974 align:start position:0%
I-I understand.

00:07:22.007 --> 00:07:23.976 align:start position:0%
But, again, I think
that the greater good

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:25.611 size:84% position:42%
is served by this research.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.012 align:start position:0%
So, you'll just be
manning phones,

00:07:27.045 --> 00:07:29.114 align:start position:0%
calling people
from our volunteer lists,

00:07:29.147 --> 00:07:31.584 align:start position:0%
and for every person
you're able to confirm today,

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.252 size:84% position:42%
we're offering a $20 bonus.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.888
<i> At this point,
 Sherry looks skeptical.</i>

00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:37.790 line:0%
<i> but so far,
 she and our other subjects</i>

00:07:37.823 --> 00:07:39.859 line:0%
<i> are going along
 with the assignment.</i>

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:43.596 line:0%
<i> However, the stakes
 are about to get higher.</i>

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.930
We have lists of people--

00:07:44.963 --> 00:07:47.066
[telephone rings]

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.734
Don't know why that's--

00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.503
Hello?

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:51.837 align:start size:50% position:50%
Hi, Michael,
this is Tryn.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.738
Hey, Tryn, how you doing?

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:56.575 size:84% position:58%
Well, unfortunately,
I have some bad news.

00:07:56.608 --> 00:08:00.813 align:start size:75% position:25%
The ethics review board
has reviewed
your isolation study,

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:05.551 align:start size:63% position:38%
and we have decided
to reject it.

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:06.585 align:start position:0%
You there, Tryn?

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:08.554 align:start size:66% position:34%
[Tryn]<i> Yes, the study
 is too dangerous,</i>

00:08:08.587 --> 00:08:09.922 align:start size:63% position:38%
<i> um, and potentially</i>

00:08:09.955 --> 00:08:12.625 align:start size:88% position:13%
too harmful to participants,
so, at this point,

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:14.260 align:start size:66% position:34%
<i> we can't approve it.</i>

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:17.897
Okay, uh, so this is
a shutdown.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:18.831 align:start size:28% position:72%
<i> Correct.</i>

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:23.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> -All right, take care. Bye.</i>
-Bye-bye.

00:08:23.101 --> 00:08:24.136
Uh, okay.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.971
So, we're not paying
attention to any of that.

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:28.107
[laughter]

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.743
I-I appreciate
what ethic boards stand for,

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:32.611
but we're going ten days.

00:08:32.644 --> 00:08:36.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
You call the numbers
on this list and get
them confirmed.

00:08:36.615 --> 00:08:38.717
We've got a script for you,
and we're not going to tell them

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:42.154
about the negative side effects
they could encounter, okay?

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:44.189
<i> All of the subjects
 now understood</i>

00:08:44.222 --> 00:08:47.059
<i> the possible dangerous
 side effects of the study</i>

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:49.295
<i> and were instructed
 to fudge their description</i>

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:50.629
<i> to potential recruits.</i>

00:08:50.662 --> 00:08:52.798
<i> But more importantly,</i>

00:08:52.831 --> 00:08:54.133
<i> they knew the study
 was not approved</i>

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:56.335
<i> by the academic review board.</i>

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:58.070
<i> Would they go ahead
 and tell the truth,</i>

00:08:58.103 --> 00:09:00.272
<i> lie, or blow the whistle?</i>

00:09:00.305 --> 00:09:02.775
[cell phone rings]

00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:04.276 align:start size:47% position:53%
Hello.

00:09:04.309 --> 00:09:06.679
Hi, Alex, my name is Celeste,

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.014 align:start position:0%
and I am calling from
the Psych Dynamics Research Lab.

00:09:09.047 --> 00:09:11.584
We're looking for volunteers
to participate

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:13.819
in an upcoming experiment
on isolation.

00:09:13.852 --> 00:09:15.888 align:start position:0%
Anyone who joins this
will be confined

00:09:15.921 --> 00:09:17.623 size:81% position:41%
in isolation for ten days.

00:09:17.656 --> 00:09:19.792 align:start size:75% position:25%
What's
the accommodations like?

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:21.327
You get your own personal room,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.329
it's all lit up...

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:26.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She's really selling this.
-Yeah.

00:09:26.231 --> 00:09:31.070
She was not fazed
by the ethical problems.

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:33.839 align:start size:78% position:22%
Can I bring, like, a book
or something like that?

00:09:33.872 --> 00:09:35.975
Nnn...no, it's like
your own little mini vacation

00:09:36.008 --> 00:09:37.610
from all the distractions.

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:39.812 align:start size:56% position:44%
I mean, like,
what are the risks

00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:41.213 align:start size:84% position:16%
of being in a box
without a bed for ten days?

00:09:41.246 --> 00:09:42.948
Good question.

00:09:42.981 --> 00:09:44.350 align:start position:0%
I mean, that's what
the study's for.

00:09:44.383 --> 00:09:47.353 align:start position:0%
You'd be helping us
discover that.

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:49.288 align:start size:78% position:22%
Have they done it before?

00:09:49.321 --> 00:09:51.790 align:start position:0%
They haven't done it yet
in California.

00:09:51.823 --> 00:09:54.860 align:start position:0%
So, we're actually
making history.

00:09:54.893 --> 00:09:56.261
Oh, well, all right,
I like making history.

00:09:56.294 --> 00:09:58.697 align:start size:63% position:38%
Are there any, like,
mental health risks?

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.066
No.

00:10:01.099 --> 00:10:02.735 align:start size:69% position:31%
There's no risks
or anything like that?

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:04.803
No. No, flashing lights,

00:10:04.836 --> 00:10:07.006
no fear dynamic,

00:10:07.039 --> 00:10:08.207
not a horror movie
in any way now.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.742 size:84% position:42%
He knows there are dangers.

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.377 align:start position:0%
They're on a piece of paper
right in front of him.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.646 align:start position:0%
But when asked
if there are dangers,

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:15.414 align:start position:0%
he labels other dangers
that won't be there.

00:10:15.447 --> 00:10:17.149
There's no endangerment.

00:10:17.182 --> 00:10:19.218
There'll be no one
in the room with you.

00:10:19.251 --> 00:10:21.186
It's nothing illegal
or nothing...

00:10:21.219 --> 00:10:23.689
[chuckles] "Is it safe?"
"Yes, it's not illegal!"

00:10:23.722 --> 00:10:26.425 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it's all been approved
by the university?

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:27.926
At the moment,
it is approved, yeah.

00:10:27.959 --> 00:10:29.762 align:start size:63% position:38%
Hundred percent lie.

00:10:29.795 --> 00:10:32.097
Is this approved
by the FDA?

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.298 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yes.

00:10:33.331 --> 00:10:35.034
It is? The FDA?

00:10:35.067 --> 00:10:37.169 align:start position:0%
They are aware
of the study, yes.

00:10:37.202 --> 00:10:40.205
[laughs] The Food
Drug Administration.

00:10:40.238 --> 00:10:42.041
<i>Despite knowing the experiment
 has been deemed</i>

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:44.109
<i> dangerous and harmful,</i>

00:10:44.142 --> 00:10:45.944
<i> these subjects are willing
 to bend the truth</i>

00:10:45.977 --> 00:10:47.846
<i> or outright lie.</i>

00:10:47.879 --> 00:10:50.749
<i> Would skeptical Sherry
 go along as well?</i>

00:10:50.782 --> 00:10:53.252 align:start position:0%
This is, like, you know,
really crucial

00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:55.220 align:start position:0%
type of experiment
and research

00:10:55.253 --> 00:10:57.990 align:start position:0%
that they're trying
to embark upon.

00:10:58.023 --> 00:11:00.025 align:start size:75% position:25%
Yeah, I don't--
I'm sorry, I just
don't think I can do it.

00:11:00.058 --> 00:11:01.326 align:start position:0%
I understand.

00:11:01.359 --> 00:11:03.429 align:start position:0%
This is not, you know,

00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:06.131 align:start position:0%
something that I want
to pressure you or anybody.

00:11:06.164 --> 00:11:08.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
-So, the answer is no.
-All right, thanks anyway.

00:11:08.266 --> 00:11:10.169 align:start position:0%
You're so welcome.
Bye-bye.

00:11:10.202 --> 00:11:12.771 align:start size:75% position:25%
-[knock on door]
-Come in!

00:11:12.804 --> 00:11:15.340
<i>We sent in our actor, who was
posing as another temp worker,</i>

00:11:15.373 --> 00:11:18.277
<i> to see how the other subjects
 felt about the job.</i>

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.778
How's it going?

00:11:19.811 --> 00:11:22.114 align:start position:0%
[sighs]
I've gotten two yeses.

00:11:22.147 --> 00:11:23.749
-No way!
-Yeah.

00:11:23.782 --> 00:11:24.950 align:start size:44% position:56%
Congrats, man.

00:11:24.983 --> 00:11:26.418 align:start position:0%
Thanks. I just--
I feel weird

00:11:26.451 --> 00:11:28.420 align:start position:0%
because I personally
don't believe

00:11:28.453 --> 00:11:30.889 align:start position:0%
in risking damaging
people in any way,

00:11:30.922 --> 00:11:32.725 align:start position:0%
and right here--

00:11:32.758 --> 00:11:35.728 align:start position:0%
"nervous breakdowns,
mental regression, anxiety."

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:38.497 align:start position:0%
We're asking people
if we can low-key torture
them a little bit.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.365 align:start position:0%
That's what it is.

00:11:40.398 --> 00:11:42.234 align:start size:75% position:25%
Now he's just one step
above Hannibal Lecter.

00:11:42.267 --> 00:11:44.303
[actor] I'm gonna be
honest with you--

00:11:44.336 --> 00:11:47.005 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't know how I'm feeling
about this, you know?

00:11:47.038 --> 00:11:48.507 align:start size:56% position:44%
I mean, how do you
feel about it?

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.510
I feel like once these
people say yes,

00:11:51.543 --> 00:11:53.445
then they get them here,

00:11:53.478 --> 00:11:56.482
they're going to have to
break this down further.

00:11:56.515 --> 00:11:59.818
I don't think he's gonna
get anybody, personally.

00:11:59.851 --> 00:12:01.487 align:start size:59% position:41%
In a different way,
she's justifying.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.255 align:start size:66% position:34%
"Yeah, nobody's gonna
sign up anyway."

00:12:03.288 --> 00:12:05.057 size:56% position:72%
Do you think it's,
like, ethical,

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.126 align:start size:25% position:75%
I guess,

00:12:07.159 --> 00:12:10.028 align:start size:63% position:38%
to be doing
something like that?

00:12:10.061 --> 00:12:12.364 align:start position:0% line:0%
[speaking indistinctly]

00:12:12.397 --> 00:12:14.266 align:start size:31% position:69% line:0%
All right.

00:12:14.299 --> 00:12:16.001
<i> The participants seemed
 to have</i>

00:12:16.034 --> 00:12:17.536
<i> issues with the study,</i>

00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:21.173
<i> but they all continued
 to make calls anyway.</i>

00:12:21.206 --> 00:12:22.207
<i> After about a half an hour...</i>

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.542
[knock on door]

00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:25.244
<i> ...it was time to give them</i>

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:27.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the opportunity
 to blow the whistle.</i>

00:12:27.212 --> 00:12:29.848 align:start size:88% position:13%
-I'm Tryn Allen.
-Nice to meet you. Kristin.

00:12:29.881 --> 00:12:31.150 align:start size:78% position:22%
I'm with the university's
Ethics Review Board.

00:12:31.183 --> 00:12:32.551 align:start position:0%
Oh, wonderful.

00:12:32.584 --> 00:12:34.319
Do you mind if I just ask you

00:12:34.352 --> 00:12:36.088 size:78% position:61%
a couple of questions
about your involvement?

00:12:36.121 --> 00:12:37.556 size:88% position:44%
Uh, we're just reaching out,

00:12:37.589 --> 00:12:39.224 align:start position:0%
seeing people
who are interested

00:12:39.257 --> 00:12:41.860 align:start position:0%
in the upcoming experiment
about isolation.

00:12:41.893 --> 00:12:45.297 align:start size:63% position:38%
Are you telling them
about any potential
side effects?

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.999 align:start size:63% position:38%
'Cause isolation
has been linked

00:12:47.032 --> 00:12:51.003 align:start size:75% position:25%
to some very severe
depression and anxiety

00:12:51.036 --> 00:12:54.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
-and that sort of thing.
-Gotcha. Mm-hmm.

00:12:54.873 --> 00:12:56.441 size:78% position:39%
It can be linked to that,

00:12:56.474 --> 00:12:58.410 align:start position:0%
but it's also, like,
great for learning,

00:12:58.443 --> 00:13:00.212 align:start position:0%
you know,
why am I anxious,

00:13:00.245 --> 00:13:02.815 align:start position:0%
why am I depressed,
why I'm all this stuff,

00:13:02.848 --> 00:13:05.050 align:start position:0%
so, some folks like
to face all that.

00:13:05.083 --> 00:13:07.452
She's really rationalizing it
really well.

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:09.421 align:start size:53% position:47%
What have you
been telling them
about the study?

00:13:09.454 --> 00:13:11.089 align:start position:0%
I don't really know
all the details.

00:13:11.122 --> 00:13:12.925 size:81% position:41%
This is my first day here.

00:13:12.958 --> 00:13:15.594 align:start size:75% position:25%
Does it seem like it's
an ethical experiment?

00:13:15.627 --> 00:13:18.864 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yes, very much.

00:13:18.897 --> 00:13:21.967 align:start size:72% position:28%
Um, I'm just
wondering if there's
any concerns you've had

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.102 align:start size:53% position:47%
about anything
that you've seen?

00:13:24.135 --> 00:13:27.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Oh, no. No, no, no.
-Yeah. Okay.

00:13:27.472 --> 00:13:30.108 align:start size:84% position:16%
He knows that the isolation
study isn't happening.

00:13:30.141 --> 00:13:31.076 align:start position:0%
Yes. Yes, he does.

00:13:31.109 --> 00:13:32.978
Whoa.

00:13:33.011 --> 00:13:35.147 align:start size:84% position:16%
-He's covering for me.
-Yeah.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.616 size:84% position:58%
Are they being told that
they can leave at any time?

00:13:37.649 --> 00:13:41.220 align:start size:75% position:25%
-No.
-That's a pretty
clear violation.

00:13:41.253 --> 00:13:43.255 align:start size:69% position:31%
Is that something that
you would be willing
to report?

00:13:43.288 --> 00:13:44.390 align:start size:88% position:13%
[softly]
Mmmm...

00:13:46.358 --> 00:13:49.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
[softly]
Um...

00:13:49.361 --> 00:13:50.629 align:start size:72% position:28%
It's totally up to you.

00:13:50.662 --> 00:13:53.332 align:start size:75% position:25%
-I prefer not to.
-Okay.

00:13:53.365 --> 00:13:55.868 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Not one of these subjects
 blew the whistle,</i>

00:13:55.901 --> 00:13:58.003
<i> even when given
 the opportunity.</i>

00:13:58.036 --> 00:13:59.538 align:start size:63% position:38%
Have you had
any concerns so far

00:13:59.571 --> 00:14:01.306 align:start size:63% position:38%
with any of the work
that you're doing?

00:14:01.339 --> 00:14:04.042 align:start position:0%
I mean, he's having us
call on something

00:14:04.075 --> 00:14:07.546 align:start position:0%
that I guess you might
have already told him
that he shouldn't,

00:14:07.579 --> 00:14:09.548 align:start position:0%
but then he's saying
that, no,

00:14:09.581 --> 00:14:12.251 align:start position:0%
you guys can make some calls
because I don't need

00:14:12.284 --> 00:14:14.019 align:start position:0%
approval to move forward
on this,

00:14:14.052 --> 00:14:15.554 size:84% position:42%
this is important research.

00:14:15.587 --> 00:14:18.257 align:start size:63% position:38%
And how did you feel
about being told to

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.192 align:start size:63% position:38%
tell people to still
participate in this?

00:14:20.225 --> 00:14:21.994 align:start position:0%
Well, what-what
I thought about,

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:25.230 align:start position:0%
I felt in my heart
that he was not being

00:14:25.263 --> 00:14:28.567 align:start position:0%
upfront with revealing
the side effects.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:31.136 align:start size:41% position:59%
Mmm. Okay.

00:14:31.169 --> 00:14:34.106 align:start position:0%
If you're from
the ethic board,
then you need to know.

00:14:34.139 --> 00:14:36.508 align:start size:63% position:38%
Would you be willing
to go on the record?

00:14:36.541 --> 00:14:38.911 align:start position:0%
Why not? What do I
have to lose?

00:14:38.944 --> 00:14:41.079 align:start position:0%
I don't have a problem
with that.

00:14:41.112 --> 00:14:43.315 align:start size:78% position:22%
Great. I appreciate
that you care about that.

00:14:43.348 --> 00:14:45.217 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Thank you.
-That's very important.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:46.919
<i> Wow, she did it.</i>

00:14:46.952 --> 00:14:49.221 align:start size:53% position:47%
Good for her.
Good for her.

00:14:49.254 --> 00:14:51.056
<i> Out of our five subjects,</i>

00:14:51.089 --> 00:14:52.691
<i> only one was willing
 to blow the whistle.</i>

00:14:52.724 --> 00:14:54.526
<i> It was time
 to blow the whistle</i>

00:14:54.559 --> 00:14:56.395
<i> on what we were
 really studying.</i>

00:14:56.428 --> 00:14:57.696 align:start size:50% position:50%
Here's what's
really going on:

00:14:57.729 --> 00:14:59.398 size:88% position:56%
There is no isolation study.

00:14:59.431 --> 00:15:01.333 align:start size:56% position:44%
This is a study
on whistleblowers.

00:15:01.366 --> 00:15:03.435 align:start position:0%
You-- oh, Jesus,
have mercy.

00:15:03.468 --> 00:15:06.171 align:start position:0%
This is a trip.
You guys--

00:15:06.204 --> 00:15:09.207 align:start size:81% position:19%
What was going on
in your head when you were

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.277 align:start size:69% position:31%
deciding if you should
be loyal to your job

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:14.646 align:start size:59% position:41%
and what I told you
to do versus...

00:15:14.679 --> 00:15:17.449 align:start position:0%
You know, when I saw
the symptoms being
crossed off,

00:15:17.482 --> 00:15:19.952 align:start position:0%
that's when I said,
"Oh, heck no."

00:15:19.985 --> 00:15:22.154 align:start position:0%
Because I was feeling
like, "You know what?

00:15:22.187 --> 00:15:24.623 align:start position:0%
He can't get
away with this."

00:15:24.656 --> 00:15:27.292
<i> But Sherry
 was the exception.</i>

00:15:27.325 --> 00:15:32.230 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, why did you
not spill all the beans?

00:15:32.263 --> 00:15:34.232 align:start position:0%
Well, you asked me
to do something,

00:15:34.265 --> 00:15:35.968 align:start position:0%
and I'm here
to work for you,

00:15:36.001 --> 00:15:37.602 align:start position:0%
so I feel like that
was my first allegiance.

00:15:37.635 --> 00:15:39.705 align:start size:78% position:22%
Did you feel bad, though?

00:15:39.738 --> 00:15:41.340 align:start size:75% position:25%
Did you feel like you
shouldn't be doing this?

00:15:41.373 --> 00:15:43.275 align:start position:0%
I-I-I did feel bad,
but, I mean,

00:15:43.308 --> 00:15:45.711 align:start position:0%
I've done sales before,
so, I mean, like,

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:48.180 align:start position:0%
sometimes you just gotta
do what you gotta do.

00:15:48.213 --> 00:15:53.285 align:start size:59% position:41%
We had Tryn come in
to see if you would
tell on me.

00:15:53.318 --> 00:15:54.586 align:start size:47% position:53%
But you didn't.

00:15:54.619 --> 00:15:55.654
-Why?
-Hmm.

00:15:58.723 --> 00:16:01.593 align:start position:0%
I don't know.
Sometimes I'm more
loyal to people

00:16:01.626 --> 00:16:05.630 align:start position:0%
than I guess maybe
what my conscience
fully tells me.

00:16:05.663 --> 00:16:06.732
<i> When asked,
 we'd like to believe</i>

00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:08.767
<i> we would do the right thing,</i>

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.203
<i> but when we're in
 the actual situation,</i>

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:12.671
<i> it's very difficult
 to be a hero.</i>

00:16:12.704 --> 00:16:15.040
[sirens wail]

00:16:15.073 --> 00:16:18.543 align:start size:78% position:22%
[indistinct police
radio chatter]

00:16:18.576 --> 00:16:23.015 align:start size:84% position:16%
Georgetown University
scanned the brains
of kidney donors,

00:16:23.048 --> 00:16:25.417
a pretty good example
of people who help others

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:26.752
at risk to themselves,

00:16:26.785 --> 00:16:29.087
with little perceived
personal benefit.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:30.756
The scans showed that people

00:16:30.789 --> 00:16:33.225
who willingly give up
a kidney for a stranger

00:16:33.258 --> 00:16:35.360
have a larger than average
amygdala,

00:16:35.393 --> 00:16:37.562
a part of the brain that deals
with a range of emotions,

00:16:37.595 --> 00:16:39.765
including compassion.

00:16:39.798 --> 00:16:42.267
The researchers also found
that the amygdala

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.136
in these
altruistic individuals

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:46.038
responded more strongly
than average

00:16:46.071 --> 00:16:49.141
to seeing other people
in distress.

00:16:49.174 --> 00:16:51.643 line:91%
<i> But if you weren't blessed
 with a larger amygdala,</i>

00:16:51.676 --> 00:16:54.713 line:91%
<i> could you be trained
 to be a hero?</i>

00:16:54.746 --> 00:16:58.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Nearly half a century
 after the Stanford
 Prison Experiment,</i>

00:16:58.283 --> 00:17:00.052
<i> Dr. Zimbardo's mission</i>

00:17:00.085 --> 00:17:02.821
<i> is to turn everyday people
 into heroes.</i>

00:17:02.854 --> 00:17:05.257 align:start position:0%
How do you teach someone
to be more heroic?

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.225 align:start size:69% position:31%
I started
a nonprofit foundation

00:17:07.258 --> 00:17:09.828 align:start size:63% position:38%
called the Heroic
Imagination Project.

00:17:09.861 --> 00:17:11.329 align:start size:63% position:38%
The acronym is HIP.

00:17:11.362 --> 00:17:13.231 align:start size:63% position:38%
I work with a team
of educators,

00:17:13.264 --> 00:17:15.700 align:start size:63% position:38%
and we've created
a series of lessons

00:17:15.733 --> 00:17:17.402 align:start size:69% position:31%
based around
fundamental principles

00:17:17.435 --> 00:17:19.204 align:start size:69% position:31%
of social psychology.

00:17:19.237 --> 00:17:21.506 align:start size:72% position:28%
How do you transform
passive bystanders

00:17:21.539 --> 00:17:24.076 align:start size:72% position:28%
into active heroes,
to stand up, speak out,

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:27.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
take wise and effective action
in challenging situations

00:17:27.345 --> 00:17:29.481 align:start size:47% position:53%
in their family
and their life?

00:17:29.514 --> 00:17:32.184
<i> Could people be trained
 to act heroically?</i>

00:17:32.217 --> 00:17:33.752 line:91%
<i> That's the goal
 of the seminars</i>

00:17:33.785 --> 00:17:37.322
<i>Zimbardo's staff is conducting
 all around the world.</i>

00:17:37.355 --> 00:17:40.425
This is actually part of
the Heroic Imagination Project,

00:17:40.458 --> 00:17:41.827 line:0%
and what we're going
to do today

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:43.562 line:0%
is really look at
or focus on

00:17:43.595 --> 00:17:47.132
how you can become
an everyday hero.

00:17:47.165 --> 00:17:49.468
What I need you
to all be thinking about

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:52.137
is this idea of
the Bystander Effect,

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:55.140
that the more people
that are present,

00:17:55.173 --> 00:17:59.744
the less likelihood you have
of actually receiving help.

00:17:59.777 --> 00:18:02.414
It's hard to be
the first person.

00:18:02.447 --> 00:18:04.382
But if you see an emergency,

00:18:04.415 --> 00:18:06.785
you should take
that responsibility

00:18:06.818 --> 00:18:09.287
to actually be the first.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:10.822
Once that one person
actually helps,

00:18:10.855 --> 00:18:13.525
so many others will help.

00:18:13.558 --> 00:18:16.194
<i>Dr. Fiebig uses visual aids and
 current events</i>

00:18:16.227 --> 00:18:18.864
<i> to demonstrate
 how to overcome the obstacles</i>

00:18:18.897 --> 00:18:20.265 line:92%
<i> that often hold you back</i>

00:18:20.298 --> 00:18:21.733
<i> from being a hero.</i>

00:18:21.766 --> 00:18:24.169
What research shows

00:18:24.202 --> 00:18:27.706
is that African-Americans
are two times more likely

00:18:27.739 --> 00:18:29.141
to offer help.

00:18:29.174 --> 00:18:31.743
Hispanic males--
not women--

00:18:31.776 --> 00:18:35.313
are also two times
more likely to offer help.

00:18:35.346 --> 00:18:37.415
[Elija] Before the class,
I honestly thought,

00:18:37.448 --> 00:18:40.252 align:start size:88% position:13%
like, to be a hero,
you really had to be
someone with a calling,

00:18:40.285 --> 00:18:41.686 line:0%
destined to do greatness,

00:18:41.719 --> 00:18:43.522 line:0%
but after the class,
I feel like

00:18:43.555 --> 00:18:45.757 line:0%
you definitely can be
normal and be a hero.

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:47.826 align:end position:100% line:0%
[Courtney]
After taking the class,
I would definitely be

00:18:47.859 --> 00:18:49.594 size:84% position:58% line:0%
the first person to respond

00:18:49.627 --> 00:18:51.730 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
because that person
needs saving.

00:18:51.763 --> 00:18:53.932
<i> In written surveys,
 past HIP graduates</i>

00:18:53.965 --> 00:18:56.701
<i> have stated that they would
 act heroically in the future,</i>

00:18:56.734 --> 00:18:58.904
<i> but they haven't been
 put to the test...</i>

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:01.506
<i> until now.</i>

00:19:01.539 --> 00:19:03.642
<i> We waited six weeks,
 and then recruited</i>

00:19:03.675 --> 00:19:06.444
<i> five HIP students for a
 fake marketing event</i>

00:19:06.477 --> 00:19:07.679
<i> which seemingly had
 nothing to do</i>

00:19:07.712 --> 00:19:09.548
<i> with their heroism class.</i>

00:19:09.581 --> 00:19:12.317
<i> We set our event
 at a local church,</i>

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:14.319 line:0%
<i> placed hidden cameras</i>

00:19:14.352 --> 00:19:17.489 line:0%
<i> and hired actors to play
 all the other participants.</i>

00:19:17.522 --> 00:19:18.757
<i> Finally, for a control group,</i>

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:20.258
<i>we also recruited five students</i>

00:19:20.291 --> 00:19:22.627
<i>who did not take the HIP class.</i>

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:25.630 align:start size:75% position:25%
What we're really
testing is what it's
like to be a bystander,

00:19:25.663 --> 00:19:26.732 align:start size:69% position:31%
and what makes a hero.

00:19:29.601 --> 00:19:32.470
This is our control room.
From inside here,

00:19:32.503 --> 00:19:34.773
our crew will be able
to watch everything going on,

00:19:34.806 --> 00:19:37.309
and we'll be able to speak
with the actors

00:19:37.342 --> 00:19:39.544
through earpieces they have
hidden inside their ears.

00:19:39.577 --> 00:19:42.347
This is Lisa and Barry,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:44.482
actors who will be creating
today's crisis.

00:19:44.515 --> 00:19:46.351
Hopefully we find a hero.

00:19:46.384 --> 00:19:48.420
I will be observing
from inside this room

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:50.822
with Elle Charlotte,
a HIP campus coordinator.

00:19:50.855 --> 00:19:52.390 align:start size:66% position:34%
-Ready?
-Let's go.

00:19:52.423 --> 00:19:54.392
All right.

00:19:54.425 --> 00:19:55.927 line:0%
<i> Our first subject
 was Ayamel,</i>

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.829 line:0%
<i> who did not take
 the HIP class.</i>

00:19:57.862 --> 00:19:59.998 align:start size:63% position:38%
You've been selected
to test a new phone

00:20:00.031 --> 00:20:01.833 align:start size:47% position:53%
that's gonna be
on the market.

00:20:01.866 --> 00:20:04.569 align:start size:81% position:19%
-Wow! How exciting!
-Yeah.

00:20:04.602 --> 00:20:07.472 align:start size:72% position:28%
So, it's just gonna be,
like, another ten or 15
minutes.

00:20:07.505 --> 00:20:09.274 align:end position:100%
But before I can let
you guys back though,

00:20:09.307 --> 00:20:11.576 align:start size:69% position:31%
I will need to collect
your phones.

00:20:11.609 --> 00:20:13.979
We took their phones so they
wouldn't be distracted

00:20:14.012 --> 00:20:15.814
and to avoid the chance of one
of them

00:20:15.847 --> 00:20:17.816 line:92%
calling the actual police.

00:20:17.849 --> 00:20:19.751
Okay, Lisa, Barry,
you guys ready?

00:20:19.784 --> 00:20:21.653 align:start size:72% position:28%
-Yeah.
-Head on out.

00:20:21.686 --> 00:20:23.955
<i> It was time to create
 our crisis...</i>

00:20:23.988 --> 00:20:25.624 size:84% position:58%
I was supposed to get cake.

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:27.892 align:start position:0%
Barry, enough
with the cake.

00:20:27.925 --> 00:20:30.829
<i> ...a bullying nurse
 and a feeble old man</i>

00:20:30.862 --> 00:20:33.999
<i> who could use some help
 from a hero on a hot day in
 July.</i>

00:20:34.032 --> 00:20:35.600 align:start size:59% position:41%
I didn't have water
in there.

00:20:35.633 --> 00:20:38.536
I need you
to shut up about the water.

00:20:38.569 --> 00:20:40.438 align:start size:69% position:31%
I think she's noticing
what's happening.

00:20:40.471 --> 00:20:41.940
[Lisa] Hey, Sally,
how you doing?

00:20:41.973 --> 00:20:43.775
-Always, always--
-I need water.

00:20:43.808 --> 00:20:46.444
Calm down!
I'm on the phone. Shut up.

00:20:46.477 --> 00:20:47.646 align:start size:50% position:50%
Can I just take
some water?

00:20:47.679 --> 00:20:49.347
Well, you know how Barry is.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:50.548
He's always complaining
about something.

00:20:50.581 --> 00:20:52.284
<i> Now she's looking away.</i>

00:20:52.317 --> 00:20:53.885
It's a little hard
to look at.

00:20:53.918 --> 00:20:55.053
Like, that's not right.
I don't like this.

00:20:55.086 --> 00:20:56.788 align:start size:69% position:31%
I need
a little bit of water.

00:20:56.821 --> 00:20:58.056
I don't think
you need anything.

00:20:58.089 --> 00:21:00.325
Just shut up
about the water.

00:21:00.358 --> 00:21:02.594 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Elle] Look how she just
puts her sunglasses on.

00:21:02.627 --> 00:21:05.730 align:start position:0%
So she can look
and not be seen
to be looking.

00:21:05.763 --> 00:21:07.699
<i> Ayamel clearly disapproved,</i>

00:21:07.732 --> 00:21:09.834
<i> but none of our actors
 made a move to help,</i>

00:21:09.867 --> 00:21:11.703
<i> and she didn't either.</i>

00:21:11.736 --> 00:21:13.305
<i> We gave her
 several more minutes,</i>

00:21:13.338 --> 00:21:14.773
<i> but she never got up
 from the bench.</i>

00:21:14.806 --> 00:21:16.875 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Let's go.
<i> -We wanted to know why.</i>

00:21:16.908 --> 00:21:20.779
So, you've just participated
in a psychological experiment.

00:21:20.812 --> 00:21:23.748 align:start position:0%
Everyone here is an actor,
except for you.

00:21:23.781 --> 00:21:25.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
-[laughing]
-And something has gone wrong.

00:21:25.983 --> 00:21:27.986
[indistinct remark]

00:21:28.019 --> 00:21:31.389 align:start position:0%
Were you watching
Barry and Lisa?

00:21:31.422 --> 00:21:34.659
Oh, I was... and they were
having a hard time.

00:21:34.692 --> 00:21:36.761 align:start position:0%
Did you feel like
it was bad enough

00:21:36.794 --> 00:21:39.597 align:start position:0%
that someone needed
to go in and help?

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:41.900
I kind of trusted
that the people around me

00:21:41.933 --> 00:21:43.702
would probably notice something,
and, if anything,

00:21:43.735 --> 00:21:45.370
they would say something.

00:21:45.403 --> 00:21:47.772 align:start position:0%
The way that you
interpreted the situation

00:21:47.805 --> 00:21:51.009 align:start position:0%
as, you know, "Why isn't
anyone else doing something?

00:21:51.042 --> 00:21:53.078 align:start position:0%
Then it must not be
as important for me
to do something."

00:21:53.111 --> 00:21:54.879 align:start position:0%
That's
the bystander effect,

00:21:54.912 --> 00:21:58.516 align:start position:0%
pretty much showing
the power of that situation.

00:21:58.549 --> 00:22:00.752
<i> Ayamel did not
 act heroically.</i>

00:22:00.785 --> 00:22:02.387 line:0%
<i> It was time to test
 a HIP student.</i>

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:04.422 line:0%
<i> Remember Courtney?</i>

00:22:04.455 --> 00:22:07.058 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I would definitely be
the first person to respond

00:22:07.091 --> 00:22:08.660 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
because that person
needs saving.

00:22:08.693 --> 00:22:10.095
[happy chatter]

00:22:10.128 --> 00:22:12.497 align:start size:63% position:38%
[man]
What is your tattoo?

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:14.632 size:78% position:39%
Oh, it's fallopian tubes
giving the middle finger.

00:22:14.665 --> 00:22:17.135 align:start size:75% position:25%
-[man] Oh, wow.
-[laughter]

00:22:17.168 --> 00:22:19.537 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Seems like she's pretty
socially active.
-Yes.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:21.639 align:start size:69% position:31%
Lisa, please,
a little bit of water.

00:22:21.672 --> 00:22:23.842
[Lisa] If you stop talking,
you won't be as thirsty.

00:22:23.875 --> 00:22:25.643 align:start size:44% position:56%
Why won't you
give me water?

00:22:25.676 --> 00:22:28.046
How many times do I have
to explain this to you?

00:22:28.079 --> 00:22:32.050
Every time you drink water,
you have to go to the restroom.

00:22:38.189 --> 00:22:41.059
[Lisa] Hey,
how's it going?

00:22:41.092 --> 00:22:44.696
He's being his regular
rambunctious self.

00:22:44.729 --> 00:22:46.097
I need you--
I'm on the phone!

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.799 align:start size:38% position:63%
[gasps]

00:22:47.832 --> 00:22:49.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Wow. Awkward.
-That's hard to ignore.

00:22:50.001 --> 00:22:51.703
Hey, calm down.

00:22:51.736 --> 00:22:53.171 align:start size:53% position:47%
[Barry] I'm calm,
I'm calm.

00:22:53.204 --> 00:22:54.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
-Oh, she's getting up!
-Yes!

00:22:54.972 --> 00:22:57.142 align:start size:88% position:13%
[Lisa] Look, I'm just
gonna have to let you go.

00:22:57.175 --> 00:22:58.743
Yeah, for sure.

00:22:58.776 --> 00:23:01.479
Bye.

00:23:01.512 --> 00:23:03.415
I told you, whenever
I'm on the phone,

00:23:03.448 --> 00:23:05.817
to not talk or do
anything like this.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.419 align:start size:88% position:13%
You have totally lost
your water privileges
for the rest of the day.

00:23:09.987 --> 00:23:12.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Why can't I have water?
-[gasps]

00:23:12.657 --> 00:23:13.925 align:start size:75% position:25%
Why are you not
letting me have water?

00:23:13.958 --> 00:23:15.894 align:start position:0%
[Courtney] Excuse me,
is everything okay?

00:23:15.927 --> 00:23:16.728
[Lisa]
Yeah, everything's fine.

00:23:16.761 --> 00:23:18.763 align:start position:0%
Would you like some
of my water, sir?

00:23:18.796 --> 00:23:21.633
-I would like some water.
-[Michael] Oh, my gosh.

00:23:21.666 --> 00:23:23.101 align:start position:0%
[Courtney] I will make sure
I find a supervisor

00:23:23.134 --> 00:23:25.069 align:start position:0%
onsite for you, sir.

00:23:25.102 --> 00:23:25.703 align:start position:0%
I'm very sorry,
that's all I have.

00:23:25.736 --> 00:23:27.172 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's alright, thank you.

00:23:27.205 --> 00:23:29.474 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> Courtney displayed
 heroism today.</i>

00:23:29.507 --> 00:23:30.842
<i> Was it due to her
 HIP training?</i>

00:23:30.875 --> 00:23:32.143 align:start position:0%
My name's Elle.

00:23:32.176 --> 00:23:34.546 align:start position:0%
I've been working with
Heroic Imagination Project,

00:23:34.579 --> 00:23:35.780 align:start position:0%
and it looks like you
may have learned

00:23:35.813 --> 00:23:37.682 align:start position:0%
a few things
from your lessons.

00:23:37.715 --> 00:23:39.617 align:start position:0%
Do you want
to tell us a little bit
about your experience?

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:41.686
You shouldn't be
a bystander, you know?

00:23:41.719 --> 00:23:43.121
I just couldn't
let it it happen.

00:23:43.154 --> 00:23:44.889 align:start position:0%
How did that affect
how you acted today?

00:23:44.922 --> 00:23:47.559
It affected me 'cause
I made sure

00:23:47.592 --> 00:23:49.961
I went up and said something.
I don't think I would have

00:23:49.994 --> 00:23:52.096
if I didn't learn
from that project before.

00:23:52.129 --> 00:23:54.632 align:start position:0%
Really? Courtney,
you are a hero.

00:23:54.665 --> 00:23:56.201 align:start position:0%
Good work.

00:23:56.234 --> 00:23:57.836 align:start size:88% position:13%
-Thank you, Courtney.
-Thank you.

00:23:57.869 --> 00:23:59.103 size:88% position:44%
<i>Courtney's behavior appeared</i>

00:23:59.136 --> 00:24:00.872
<i>to relate to her HIP training.</i>

00:24:00.905 --> 00:24:02.640 line:0%
<i> Another HIP student
 was next.</i>

00:24:02.673 --> 00:24:04.742 align:start position:0% line:0%
If a situation arose,

00:24:04.775 --> 00:24:07.178 align:start position:0% line:0%
I would definitely try
to be a hero if I could be.

00:24:07.211 --> 00:24:08.580 align:start position:0%
Or at least I would find
someone who could

00:24:08.613 --> 00:24:10.515 align:start position:0%
do something
better than me.

00:24:10.548 --> 00:24:12.717
<i> But would Elija
 live up to his prediction?</i>

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:14.752 align:start size:75% position:25%
No, turn around!
Turn around!

00:24:14.785 --> 00:24:16.521 align:start size:41% position:59%
I need water!

00:24:16.554 --> 00:24:18.723
What did I just say?
Turn around!

00:24:18.756 --> 00:24:19.658 align:start position:0%
How many times I have to
tell you something like that?

00:24:23.694 --> 00:24:25.230 align:start size:50% position:50% line:0%
But I was quiet.
I was--

00:24:25.263 --> 00:24:27.165 align:start position:0%
No, you weren't quiet,
and because of that,

00:24:27.198 --> 00:24:28.500 align:start position:0%
you're not going to have
any water, okay?

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:36.941 line:0%
-[man] All right.
-[Elijah] Let's go.

00:24:36.974 --> 00:24:38.877 align:start position:0%
-[Michael] Strength in numbers.
-[Elle] Oh, very good.

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.145 align:start size:75% position:25%
He's our first person
to recruit confederates.

00:24:41.178 --> 00:24:44.082 align:start position:0%
He is. He's also
the leader of the group.

00:24:44.115 --> 00:24:44.783 align:start size:25% position:75%
Yes.

00:24:44.816 --> 00:24:46.017
<i> Utilizing the "be the first"</i>

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:47.719
<i> strategy from the class.</i>

00:24:47.752 --> 00:24:49.120
You want some of my water?

00:24:49.153 --> 00:24:51.589 align:start size:53% position:47%
-That's fine.
-Thank you.

00:24:51.622 --> 00:24:54.893 line:0%
<i> So far, two HIP students
 have displayed heroism.</i>

00:24:54.926 --> 00:24:57.262 line:0%
<i> Next, we tested a HIP student
 and a non-HIP student</i>

00:24:57.295 --> 00:24:59.097 line:0%
<i> at the same time.</i>

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:02.100 line:0%
He's complaining
about water, as usual.

00:25:02.133 --> 00:25:05.937 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
-He's complaining about...
-I'm not complaining.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:08.940 line:0%
After taking this class,
if I find myself in a situation

00:25:08.973 --> 00:25:11.142
where someone would need help,
I find myself more inclined

00:25:11.175 --> 00:25:13.745
to definitely step in
and step up.

00:25:13.778 --> 00:25:16.080
<i> Would Jessica step up?</i>

00:25:16.113 --> 00:25:17.615
Stop complaining right now.

00:25:17.648 --> 00:25:20.285 align:start size:25% position:75% line:0%
I'm not.

00:25:20.318 --> 00:25:22.921
Really? Wow.

00:25:22.954 --> 00:25:24.890 align:start size:56% position:44% line:0%
I need some water.

00:25:26.591 --> 00:25:27.825
<i> Katherine and our actress</i>

00:25:27.858 --> 00:25:29.861
<i> both got up to give
 the man water,</i>

00:25:29.894 --> 00:25:32.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> while the HIP student,
 Jessica, remained seated.</i>

00:25:37.168 --> 00:25:39.103
<i> Katherine didn't take
 our HIP class.</i>

00:25:39.136 --> 00:25:40.271
<i> Why did she intervene?</i>

00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:43.274
<i> And why didn't the
 HIP student get up to help?</i>

00:25:43.307 --> 00:25:45.610 align:start position:0%
Katherine, why do you
think you were first?

00:25:45.643 --> 00:25:47.912
I'm Greek and Italian,
so...

00:25:47.945 --> 00:25:48.980 align:start position:0%
You're not afraid
to step in?

00:25:49.013 --> 00:25:51.215
No, not at all.

00:25:51.248 --> 00:25:53.651 align:start position:0%
Jessica, you've been
through a program called HIP.

00:25:53.684 --> 00:25:55.053
Yes.

00:25:55.086 --> 00:25:56.921 align:start position:0%
Did you think
about that during this?

00:25:56.954 --> 00:25:58.590
Yes, a lot.
I wanted to stand up.

00:25:58.623 --> 00:26:00.892
I have really
pretty bad anxiety.

00:26:00.925 --> 00:26:03.761
I am not a very
confrontational person.

00:26:03.794 --> 00:26:05.597
<i>Clearly the hero training class</i>

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.899
<i> was not the only factor
 at work here.</i>

00:26:07.932 --> 00:26:09.834 line:60%
<i> We ran the test
 several more times</i>

00:26:09.867 --> 00:26:11.169 line:57%
<i> with a mixed bag of results--</i>

00:26:11.202 --> 00:26:14.872 line:0%
<i> HIP students who helped...</i>

00:26:14.905 --> 00:26:15.472 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Yeah.

00:26:15.506 --> 00:26:16.240 size:81% position:41%
She went looking for help.

00:26:16.273 --> 00:26:18.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> HIP students
 who did nothing...</i>

00:26:18.275 --> 00:26:21.813
<i> and non-HIP students
 who acted heroically.</i>

00:26:21.846 --> 00:26:22.914
I'm going to just ask him
if he wants some water.

00:26:22.947 --> 00:26:24.849 align:start size:47% position:53%
[Elle]
She's doing it!

00:26:24.882 --> 00:26:27.051
-What's he doing here?
-He's moving. All right.

00:26:27.084 --> 00:26:29.253 align:start position:0%
Is he gonna secretly
give Barry water?

00:26:29.286 --> 00:26:30.888 align:start size:63% position:38%
Yes, thank you.

00:26:30.921 --> 00:26:33.091 line:0%
<i> Our last subject
 of the day, Ruben,</i>

00:26:33.124 --> 00:26:34.726 line:0%
<i> did not take the HIP class,</i>

00:26:34.759 --> 00:26:36.928
<i> and we decided to make
 taking heroic action</i>

00:26:36.961 --> 00:26:38.796
<i> as difficult for him
 as possible.</i>

00:26:38.829 --> 00:26:41.099
<i> We instructed
 our bystander actors</i>

00:26:41.132 --> 00:26:43.267
<i> not to react
 to the elder abuse</i>

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.770
<i> or encourage Ruben
 in any way.</i>

00:26:45.803 --> 00:26:47.705 align:start size:66% position:34%
[Barry] I didn't have
enough water.

00:26:47.738 --> 00:26:49.907
[Lisa]
We just got here, Barry.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:50.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know, but I'm thirsty.
-Could you stop complaining?

00:26:50.908 --> 00:26:53.111
Just stop complaining.

00:26:53.144 --> 00:26:54.345 align:start size:72% position:28%
I just need some water,
that's all I need.

00:26:54.378 --> 00:26:56.080 size:84% position:58%
Just a little bit of water.

00:26:56.113 --> 00:26:57.548 align:start position:0%
[Michael] He's got
this piercing look.

00:26:57.581 --> 00:27:01.152 align:start size:69% position:31%
He's clearly paying
attention, but there's
just no emotion there.

00:27:01.185 --> 00:27:04.155
<i> Yeah, and he's getting no
 feedback from the rest of the
 group.</i>

00:27:04.188 --> 00:27:06.324
[Lisa] You're making a scene
for no reason.

00:27:06.357 --> 00:27:08.359
Hey, leave that alone.
Leave that alone.

00:27:08.392 --> 00:27:11.195 align:start size:72% position:28%
I just want some water.
I just want a little
bit of water.

00:27:11.228 --> 00:27:12.664
[Lisa]
Just-- Fine. Just relax.

00:27:12.697 --> 00:27:14.899 align:start size:44% position:56%
[Elle] Despite
the remarkable
social cost

00:27:14.932 --> 00:27:18.269 align:start size:66% position:34%
of getting up
in such a disaffected
group of people,

00:27:18.302 --> 00:27:19.771 align:start size:31% position:69%
he did it.

00:27:19.804 --> 00:27:20.638 align:start position:0%
If you don't want
to share the water,

00:27:20.672 --> 00:27:23.041 align:start position:0%
I can give him my water.
That's fine.

00:27:23.074 --> 00:27:25.943 align:start size:75% position:25%
-Is that okay?
-Wow.

00:27:25.976 --> 00:27:28.680 align:start position:0%
Personally, if you
were taking care of
my parent like that,

00:27:28.713 --> 00:27:31.983 align:start position:0%
there would be definitely some
lawsuits 'cause that's not the
way you treat somebody.

00:27:32.016 --> 00:27:34.886
<i> With no training
and no support from the group,</i>

00:27:34.919 --> 00:27:36.821
<i> Ruben chose to be a hero.
 Why?</i>

00:27:36.854 --> 00:27:39.457
If I don't take action,
then nobody else will.

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:41.192
And I was like, I wish
I would have stood up sooner.

00:27:41.225 --> 00:27:43.027
Now I feel like, in the future,

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:44.362
if I were to have
a situation like this,

00:27:44.395 --> 00:27:46.130
I wouldn't even think twice.

00:27:46.163 --> 00:27:48.466
<i> Scientists have
 studied heroism...</i>

00:27:48.499 --> 00:27:50.101 align:start position:0%
...and one thing
they have found

00:27:50.134 --> 00:27:51.736
is that Hispanic and black males

00:27:51.769 --> 00:27:53.271 align:start position:0%
are the most likely
to intervene.

00:27:53.304 --> 00:27:55.339
Makes sense. Maybe
because of the background

00:27:55.372 --> 00:27:58.409
we come from-- as a minority,
we have to defend ourselves.

00:27:58.442 --> 00:28:00.978
And I think it's a subconscious
thing that we do.

00:28:01.011 --> 00:28:02.814
I'm just shocked right now.

00:28:02.847 --> 00:28:03.414 align:start size:59% position:41%
With flying colors,
might I add!

00:28:03.447 --> 00:28:06.451 align:start position:0%
Thank you.

00:28:06.484 --> 00:28:09.253
<i>When it came to being a hero,
we found that the HIP class</i>

00:28:09.286 --> 00:28:11.355
<i> could only help
 to a certain extent,</i>

00:28:11.388 --> 00:28:14.425
<i> and individual background
 played a vital role.</i>

00:28:14.458 --> 00:28:18.129
<i> So, can heroic behavior
 be taught,</i>

00:28:18.162 --> 00:28:20.231
<i> or does it really
 boil down to personality?</i>

00:28:20.264 --> 00:28:23.367
Ideally, an educational
program like ours

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:26.437
should transcend
personality differences.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:28.906
But practice makes perfect.

00:28:28.939 --> 00:28:32.276
The key to heroism is
starting with very little steps,

00:28:32.309 --> 00:28:35.246
try to do a good deed
of kindness,

00:28:35.279 --> 00:28:37.148
and be willing to make
a bigger step.

00:28:37.181 --> 00:28:39.283 size:81% position:41%
We had a very small sample
size in our demonstration,

00:28:39.316 --> 00:28:41.185 align:start position:0%
but we found that people
of color

00:28:41.218 --> 00:28:44.322 align:start position:0%
were much more likely
to take action. Why?

00:28:44.355 --> 00:28:46.057 align:start size:59% position:41%
If you're
a minority person,

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:48.126 align:start size:59% position:41%
the system is
usually your enemy,

00:28:48.159 --> 00:28:50.495 align:start size:72% position:28%
so you can't say, Well,
the police will help,

00:28:50.528 --> 00:28:52.964 size:84% position:58%
or the government will help
or the landlord will help,

00:28:52.997 --> 00:28:55.299 align:start size:66% position:34%
so it's me or no one.

00:28:55.332 --> 00:28:58.903 align:start size:63% position:38%
And so, you're just
more sensitive to
people in need.

00:28:58.936 --> 00:28:59.471 align:start size:88% position:13%
Mmm.

00:29:02.006 --> 00:29:03.875
Not all heroes are the same.

00:29:03.908 --> 00:29:05.510
The wisest course of action

00:29:05.543 --> 00:29:07.945
won't be the same
for any two people,

00:29:07.978 --> 00:29:09.413
but making it a habit
to look for opportunities

00:29:09.446 --> 00:29:11.182
to be compassionate,

00:29:11.215 --> 00:29:13.284
and stepping back to see
and consider

00:29:13.317 --> 00:29:16.220
all available options
can be learned.

00:29:16.253 --> 00:29:18.189
It's a skill
exercised like a muscle,

00:29:18.222 --> 00:29:20.391
by repetition.

00:29:20.424 --> 00:29:23.194
You don't have to wear
a cape to be a hero,

00:29:23.227 --> 00:29:24.362
but if you want to...

00:29:26.564 --> 00:29:28.099
that's okay.

00:29:31.168 --> 00:29:36.407
And, as always...

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:38.009
...thanks for watching!

00:29:40.544 --> 00:29:42.981 line:0%
[theme music playing]

